--
-- Test Description
-- This tests all the destination address recognition modes of the GEM in 
-- 10 or 100 mode.
--
-- Functional Coverage
-- � Ensures that a frame will not be copied to memory if the GEM is 
--   transmitting in half duplex mode at the time a destination address 
--   is received.
-- � Ensures that the GEM does proper external address recognition.
-- � Ensures that the GEM does proper address recognition for broadcast 
--   addressing in 10 or 100 mode.
-- � Ensures that the GEM does proper address recognition for unicast hash 
--   addressing in 10 or 100 mode.
-- � Ensures that the GEM does proper address recognition for multicast 
--   hash addressing in 10 or 100 mode.
-- � Ensure that the all of the specific address registers work 
--   properly.
--
-- Detailed Test Description
-- 1. Initialise GEM for 10 or 100-mode operation and external address 
--    match enable.
-- 2. Check status registers to ensure they have the proper value after reset.
-- 3. Write an address to specific address register one.
-- 4. Transmit a frame.
-- 5. Start receiving a frame.  (The GEM should not be able to receive a 
--    frame because the GEM is currently transmitting a frame)
-- 6. Check the interrupt status register once the frame is completely 
--    transmitted.
-- 7. Receive a frame that matches both the external address and local address.
--    (Local addresses are in the specific address registers)
-- 8. Check the interrupt status register once the frame is completely received.
--
-- External Addressing:
-- 9. Write new addresses to all the local address registers so that there 
--    is no local address match. Therefore, the next received frame will 
--    only match on external address.
-- 10. Receive a frame that will only match on external address. The 
--     frame should be accepted and written to memory.
-- 11. Check the interrupt status register once the frame is completely 
--     received.
-- 12. Repeat external address matching for each of the external address 
--     match pins.
--
-- Broadcast matching
-- 13. Disable external address matching and send a BROADCAST frame.
--     This frame should only match on the BROADCAST all ones address
--     Broadcast frames do not increment the rx multicast bucket counter
-- 14. Check the interrupt status register once the frame is completely 
--     received.
--
-- Copy all frames & broadcast:
-- 15. Enable the GEM to copy all frames and send a broadcast frame. 
--     (This frame should be copied to memory but should not set any of the 
--     address matching status bits)
-- 16. Check the interrupt status register and the receive status register 
--     once the frame is completely received.
--
-- Unicast Hash Addressing:
-- 17. Initialise the GEM for unicast hash addressing.
-- 18. Set the hash register bottom to 0 hex and only set bit 57 in the 
--     hash register top.
-- 19. Receive a frame with the destination address set to '1111aaaaaaaa".  
--     The hashing of this destination address reduces to '111001' (57 decimal)
--     or bit 57 in the hash address registers. (This frame should be stored to 
--     memory)
-- 20. Check the interrupt status register once the frame is received.
-- 21. Now set all hash indices (set all the bits in the hash registers) except
--     bit 57.
-- 22. Receive the same frame with the same destination address as in point 19. 
--     (This frame should be discarded.)
-- 23. Receive a broadcast frame. (The multicast frame bit is not enabled. 
--     Therefore, this frame should be discarded because it's a type of 
--     multicast frame.)
-- 25. Receive a frame with the destination address set to 'aaaa22222222'. 
--     The hashing of this destination address reduces to '001010' (10 decimal)
--     or bit 10 in the hash address registers. (This frame should be stored to
--     memory)
-- 26. Check the interrupt status register once the frame is received.
-- 27.  Set hash register bit 27 and clear all the remaining bits. (hash index 
--      of 27 decimal)
-- 28. Receive a frame with the destination address set to '1111aaaaaaaa'. 
--     The hashing of this destination address reduces to '011011' (27 decimal) 
--     or bit 27 in the hash address registers. (This frame should be stored 
--     to memory)
-- 29. Check the interrupt status register once the frame is received.
--
-- Multicast Hash Addressing:-
-- 30. Initialise the GEM for multicast hash addressing.
-- 31. Set hash register bit 5 and clear all the remaining bits. 
--     (hash index of 5 decimal)
-- 32. Receive a frame with the destination address set to 'ffffbbbbbbbb'.  
--     The hashing of this destination address reduces to '000101' (5 decimal) 
--     or bit 5 in the hash address registers. (This frame should be stored to 
--     memory)
-- 33. Check the interrupt status register once the frame is received.
-- 34. Now set all hash indices (set all the bits in the hash registers) except 
--     bit 5.
-- 35. Receive the same frame with the same destination address as in point 32. 
--     (This frame should be discarded.)
-- 36. Receive a frame with the destination address set to 'ffff77777777'. 
--     The hashing of this destination address reduces to '001010' (10 decimal)
--     or bit 10 in the hash address registers. (This frame should be stored to 
--     memory since all hash indices are enabled except 5)
-- 37. Check the interrupt status register once the frame is received.
--
-- Specific Address Register Two:
-- 38. Clear the hash registers.
-- 39. Enable the GEM for all address matching (unicast, and multicast) except 
--     for copy all frames.
-- 40. Write an address to specific address register two.
-- 41. Receive a frame that matches the local address. (Local addresses are 
--     in the specific address registers)
-- 42. Check the interrupt status register once the frame is received.

-- Specific Address Register Three:
-- 43. Write an address to specific address register three.
-- 44. Receive a frame that matches the local address. (Local addresses are
--     in the specific address registers)
-- 45. Check the interrupt status register once the frame is received.
--
-- Specific Address Register Four:
-- 46. Write an address to specific address register four.
-- 47. Receive a frame that matches the local address. (Local addresses are 
--     in the specific address registers)
-- 48. Check the interrupt status register once the frame is received.
--
-- Specific Address Register Three:
-- 49. Write an address to specific address register three.
-- 50. Receive a frame that matches the local address. (Local addresses are in 
--     the specific address registers)
-- 51. Check the interrupt status register once the frame is received.
--     Check all status and statistic registers and ensure that the above
--     operations were dealt with properly.
--
-- Type ID match register 1:
-- 52. Write a type to specific type register one.
-- 53. Receive a frame that matches the type, but no specific addresses.
-- 54. Check the interrupt status register once the frame is received.
--     Check all status registers and ensure that the above operations were 
--     dealt with properly.
--
-- Type ID match register 2:
-- 55. Write a type to specific type register two.
-- 56. Receive a frame that matches the type, but no specific addresses.
-- 57. Check the interrupt status register once the frame is received.
--     Check all status registers and ensure that the above operations were 
--     dealt with properly.
--
-- Type ID match register 3:
-- 58. Write a type to specific type register three.
-- 59. Receive a frame that matches the type, but no specific addresses.
-- 60. Check the interrupt status register once the frame is received.
--     Check all status registers and ensure that the above operations were 
--     dealt with properly.
--
-- Type ID match register 4:
-- 61. Write a type to specific type register four.
-- 62. Receive a frame that matches the type, but no specific addresses.
-- 63. Check the interrupt status register once the frame is received.
--     Check all status registers and ensure that the above operations were 
--     dealt with properly.
--
-- Two or more matches on specific address/type:
-- 64. Write the same address to specific address register one and two, and the
--     same typeID to typeID regsiters one and two.
-- 65. Receive a frame that matches the local address of 1 & 2 and the typeID
--     of 1 & 2
-- 66. Check the interrupt status register once the frame is received.
--     Check all status and statistic registers and ensure that the above
--     operations were dealt with properly.
--
-- check copy all VLAN tagged frames
-- 67. Set the "copy all frames" and "discard of Non VLAN frame" bits in the
--     Network Configuration Register.
-- 68. Receive a VLAN tagged frame which doesn't match any addresses or types
--     and ensure that it is copied to memory and the correct status reported.
-- 69. Disable the "copy all frames" bit in the Network Configuration Register.
--     but keep "discard of Non VLAN frame" set.
-- 70. Receive a VLAN tagged frame that matches specific address 2 and ensure 
--     that it is copied to memory and the correct status reported.
-- 71. Receive a VLAN tagged frame that has a broadcast address and ensure 
--     that it is copied to memory and the correct status reported.
-- 72. Receive a VLAN tagged frame that matches typeID register 1 and ensure 
--     that it is copied to memory and the correct status reported.
-- 73. Receive a VLAN tagged frame that matches on unicast hash matching and
--     ensure that it is copied to memory and the correct status reported.
-- 74. Receive a VLAN tagged frame that matches on multicast hash matching.
--     Ensure that it is copied to memory and the correct status reported.
-- 75. Receive a non-VLAN tagged frame that matches on specific address 2,
--     typeID 1 and unicast hash matching
--     Ensure that it is not copied to memory and no status is received.
-- 76. Receive a non-VLAN tagged frame with broadcast address that also matches
--     on typeID 1 and multicast hash matching.
--     Ensure that it is not copied to memory and no status is received.
-- 77. Receive a non-VLAN tagged frame that matches on typeID 1.
--     Ensure that it is not copied to memory and no status is received.
-- 78. Receive a non-VLAN tagged frame that matches on unicast hash matching.
--     Ensure that it is not copied to memory and no status is received.
-- 79. Receive a non-VLAN tagged frame that matches on multicast hash matching.
--     Ensure that it is not copied to memory and no status is received.
-- 80. Receive a VLAN tagged frame that matches on multicast hash matching.
--     Ensure that it is copied to memory and correct status is received.
-- 81. Check the statistics registers for correct operation.
--
-- Match too late timing
-- 82. Re-initialise all configuration registers for basic 100M operation
-- 83. Receive a frame and pulse the external address match one input on the
--     last clock before match_too_late signal in gem_rx is set (match_too_late 
--     is set when the new_pipeline_data signal in gem_rx is active for the 
--     first data in the frame).
-- 84. Receive a frame and pulse the external address match one input one
--     clock after the match_too_late signal in gem_rx is set.
-- 85. Receive a frame and pulse the external address match one input one
--     clock before the ext_da_stb output is activated.
--     Check that the frame is not matched, as the match signal will be outside
--     the window of the external match (starts at ext_da_stb)
-- 86. Receive a frame and pulse the external address match one input at the
--     same clock that the ext_da_stb output is activated.
--     Check that the frame is matched and copied to memory with correct 
--     status.
-- 87. Check the statistics registers for correct operation.
--
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

